---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const path = "/compare/tinyemail-vs-mailchimp";
const title = "TinyEmail vs Mailchimp for Shopify: 2026 comparison";
const desc = "TinyEmail and Mailchimp both support Shopify email. Choose around your existing audiences, data mapping and operating costs rather than an invented order-count ceiling.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does tinyEmail always cost less?",
    "answer": "Its advertised Shopify offer is free, but eligibility, send volume and migration work affect the decision. Compare that offer with the Mailchimp plan needed for your actual audiences and automations."
  },
  {
    "question": "Must I leave Mailchimp as my store grows?",
    "answer": "No. Replace it when a required workflow, operating burden or cost justifies the move. There is no supported order-count cutoff in this comparison."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a></nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="editorial-kicker">Head-to-head</p><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1><p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p></header>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Shopify support is present on both sides</h2>
      <p>tinyEmail advertises customer, product and order sync, plus cart, welcome and winback email. Mailchimp documents a Shopify connection that updates audience records with customer and order information. Calling Mailchimp incapable of ecommerce, or tinyEmail exclusive to Shopify customers, would misstate the choice. A Shopify-only program can evaluate tinyEmail without assuming that an established Mailchimp program needs replacement.</p>
      <h2>Preserve the audience model you actually use</h2>
      <p>For a store that also sends a professional newsletter, map the signup sources, subscription preferences and purchase fields before moving anything. A purchase is not permission to add somebody to every newsletter. Recreate one newsletter segment and one purchaser exclusion in the candidate platform. Keeping Mailchimp may avoid rebuilding working audience rules; moving to tinyEmail may reduce the number of separate tools used for store email, but only if those rules survive the move.</p>
      <h2>Cart content matters more than catalog size</h2>
      <p>Use a proposed rehearsal with a variant, a sold-out item and a completed purchase after cart entry. Inspect product links, images, currency and the removal of a purchaser from pending recovery messages. This is an evaluation plan, not a report of tests we ran. There is no evidence here that a particular SKU count breaks Mailchimp or that tinyEmail produces a measured recovery lift. Separate abandoned cart behavior from checkout recovery when mapping triggers.</p>
      <h2>Compare the right price basis</h2>
      <p>tinyEmail currently advertises a free Shopify offer; its FAQ lists unlimited contacts and up to one million monthly emails. Treat that as a vendor offer whose eligibility should match your store, rather than using the old paid-contact estimates on this page. For Mailchimp, price the required plan and email allowance using the current calculator. Include the effort of rebuilding templates, forms and automations; a cheaper subscription alone cannot establish migration savings.</p>
      <h2>A controlled move preserves working email</h2>
      <p>Inventory fields, suppression records, signup sources and active automations before import. Use records you can legitimately obtain, retain opt-out status and authenticate the sending domain. Build replacement messages disabled, inspect sample records, and assign one sender to each live trigger before activation. Do not enable two cart series merely to create an overlap period. Keep an owner for corrections after the first campaign.</p>
      <h2>When to keep the existing platform</h2>
      <p>Keep Mailchimp when audience organization and current automations serve the business and the alternative does not fix a named problem. Evaluate tinyEmail when its Shopify offer and editor fit the store workflow. A request for coordinated SMS, a missing trigger or an unmaintainable segment is a reason to expand the shortlist; monthly orders alone are not. Neither platform needs to be described as a temporary home.</p>
      <h2>Primary sources</h2>
      <p>Product information checked November 25, 2026. <a class="link" href="https://www.tinyemail.com/shopify-email-marketing">tinyEmail Shopify scope</a> · <a class="link" href="https://www.tinyemail.com/faqs">tinyEmail offer</a> · <a class="link" href="https://mailchimp.com/help/connect-shopify/">Mailchimp Shopify connection</a> · <a class="link" href="https://mailchimp.com/pricing/marketing/">Mailchimp pricing</a></p>
    </article>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/tinyemail-vs-shopify-email">TinyEmail vs Shopify Email</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-mailchimp">Klaviyo vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/brevo-vs-mailchimp">Brevo vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/home-goods">Email apps for home goods stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/segmentation">Segmentation playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Questions about this comparison" />
</BaseLayout>
